chase 7000  reactions then for a small coal facility  that was really a little too many  reactions for us to justify the upfront  cost and so we opted for the agilent  halo plex the agilent halo plex  technology and again we did this on the  ion proton and with 200 base pairs  sequencing the plan was to try to get  around 300 x coverage to be able to  detect rare variants and for that we  needed to use the proton so here is a  list I don't know that you can  necessarily read it but here's a list of  the 541 genes that we wanted to look at  compass a many of the known oncogenes  and tumor suppressor genes that many  cancer research investigators are  interested in so the design itself was  541 target genes which was resolved to  9800 regions when taking into account  the different different sites that we  could look at and also different  different variants as well  and so this was 186,000 articles the way  that it was designed was a paired end  design so that we could look at in both  direction for each of the genes on the  panel that's one thing that iron torrent  doesn't have the ability to do is paired  end sequencing and so this was  attractive to us to be able to do this  as well so the way that the protocol  works is the first step is to digest in  DNA to the DNA and then there is a  circularization and hybridization  reaction that allows us to hybridize the  regions of interest that we want in our  capture and then following that there's  pcr amplification to generate the  library for the sequencing and so this  reaction is very labor-intensive the  first step is setting up an eight  reaction or an 888 restriction digests  for each biological sample and so you  can only do 12 biological samples and  there will be an entire 96-well plate  and of course there's room for error as  you add these restriction enzymes and  also the master mix so really our  rationale for automating this was very  different from our rationale for  eliminating the ad be the only be we